<h1>undecidable problem</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A problem that cannot be solved for all cases by any <a href="algorithm.html"><em>algorithm</em></a> whatsoever---equivalently, whose associated <a href="language.html"><em>language</em></a> cannot be recognized by a <a href="turingMachine.html"><em>Turing machine</em></a> that halts for all inputs.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="haltingProblem.html"><em>halting problem</em></a>, <a href="postsCorrespondProb.html"><em>Post's correspondence problem</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="decidableProblem.html"><em>decidable problem</em></a>, <a href="unsolvableProblem.html"><em>unsolvable problem</em></a>, <a href="undecidableLanguage.html"><em>undecidable language</em></a>, <a href="intractable.html"><em>intractable</em></a>, <a href="busyBeaver.html"><em>busy beaver</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
From Algorithms and Theory of Computation Handbook, page 24-19 or 26-20, Copyright &copy; 1999 by CRC Press LLC. Appearing in the Dictionary of Computer Science, Engineering and Technology, Copyright &copy; 2000 CRC Press LLC.</em>
</p>

